In my current NAO game, I think I actually waited too long.

The unbounded worst case had my irrational side scared.

I'd done some computer-assisted number crunching,
based on the information in the wiki articles on Excalibur
and fountains, and I was estimating that an average
attempt to dip for Excalibur would require 5-7 dips, dry
up 2-3 fountains, get interrupted partway through by
at least one group of monsters that would have to be
fought off with a rusty longsword, and very likely incur
some other kind of complication at some point.  On top
of that, I figured I couldn't necessarily count on average
treatment from the RNG, so I decided to play it a little
bit safe.  I waited until I'd played through three levels
of Sokoban, at which point I figured I could handle
water moccasins or a nymph and would have a
decent shot at escaping the level if a water demon
showed up.  So I went to Delphi and did the dipping
there (to reduce the chances of needing to traverse
significant terrain wielding a thoroughly rusty sword;
with average luck, the four fountains in one place
would be enough).

Murphy's Law:  if I _hadn't_ waited so long, I probably
wouldn't have gotten Excalibur on the first dip.  On
this occasion, however, I did.  My relief was palpable.

After getting Excalibur, I finished up Sokoban (got the
amulet) then went for the Mines.  After some further
fiddling around (gem throwing, stash consolidation,
and whatnot, and I managed to ID a ring of teleport
control), I went back to Minetown for a round of price
identification and some protection.

I guess the next big hurdle will be Medusa.


